# Summary
Bug introduced: duplicate Content-Length headers due to manual header addition combined with streaming_body()'s own Content-Length insertion.
The GetObjectPart response builder explicitly adds a Content-Length header and then calls streaming_body, which also appends Content-Length. This results in two Content-Length headers with identical values on every part response. While the values match, multiple Content-Length headers violate HTTP/1.1 rules and can cause some clients or intermediaries to reject or mishandle the response.

Update: cleaned up in commit `4a71bcf` (`Remove duplicate GetObjectPart content length`).
This was not a real wire-level issue in the current server because final HTTP serialization
used `HeaderMap::insert`, so only one `Content-Length` header was emitted on the response.
The redundant builder-level header was still removed for clarity and future safety, and
regression tests were added for both `GetObjectPart` and `s3_response_to_hyper`.

