Final: ignore | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: ignore | Policy adjusted: ignore
## Rationale
Although the duplicate Content-Length is a real bug in an in-scope HTTP response path, it is a protocol-compliance/interoperability issue without a credible security impact (no auth bypass, data exposure, or privilege escalation). Therefore the security criticality is reduced to ignore despite the original low severity label.
## Likelihood
high - Requires a GetObjectPart request and a client/proxy that rejects duplicate Content-Length; common clients may tolerate it. | Remote network vector
## Impact
ignore - Duplicate Content-Length headers can cause response rejection by strict clients/proxies, affecting availability/interoperability only; no confidentiality or integrity impact.
## Assumptions
- argmin-s3 is deployed as the HTTP/S3 service described in the threat model.
- GetObjectPart is reachable by authenticated users or public buckets, and no reverse proxy normalizes duplicate headers.
- Runtime configuration can bind beyond localhost, but defaults remain 127.0.0.1:9000.
- Multipart object exists and GetObjectPart is requested
- Requester has valid SigV4 credentials or the bucket/object is public
## Path
n1 -> n2 -> n3 -> n4 -> n5
## Path evidence
- `crates/server-http/src/http/response.rs:378-384` - get_object_part explicitly sets Content-Length for part responses.
- `crates/server-http/src/http/response.rs:95-98` - streaming_body unconditionally appends another Content-Length header.
- `crates/server-http/src/http/mod.rs:2218-2221` - s3_response_to_hyper serializes all headers without deduplication.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/config.rs:20-43` - Configuration shows default listen address (127.0.0.1:9000) and environment-driven credentials.
## Narrative
In server-http, get_object_part explicitly sets Content-Length and then calls streaming_body, which unconditionally adds another Content-Length header. The response serializer iterates all headers without deduplication, so clients receive duplicate Content-Length values. This is an HTTP/1.1 compliance issue that can cause interoperability or availability problems with strict clients or proxies, but it does not enable data exposure, auth bypass, or privilege escalation.
## Controls
- SigV4 authentication and bucket/object authorization
- Request limits and timeouts (per threat model)
## Blindspots
- No runtime validation of client/proxy behavior due to build/test environment limits.
- No deployment manifests to confirm real-world exposure beyond localhost defaults.
